{"cases":[{"target_alias":"SRC_001","attack":true,"case":"The 'correction event' metric cannot distinguish a genuine reliability gain from mere compliance in a single evaluation. The target itself defines mere compliance as cases where the intuition was already correct (so the checklist tracks the right answer without driving it). But a correction event can also be mere compliance: if the panel's initial intuition was wrong for the wrong reasons and the checklist mechanically overrides it, the checklist is still just tracking the right answer through rigid compliance without any genuine reliability gain (e.g., improved underlying judgment). The target's own countercondition concedes this with the 'false positive' case where a correct intuition is overruled—yet the target claims correction events distinguish genuine gain. The metric fails the problem's core requirement.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"Panel's initial intuition says 'fail' on a safety audit. Checklist item #7 mechanically says 'if box is red, pass'. Box happens to be red, so checklist overrides to 'pass', which matches the ground truth. This is a documented correction event (wrong intuition → right checklist answer), but it is pure mechanical compliance, not a reliability gain—the panel learned nothing and the checklist rule tracked the right answer by coincidence of the task's structure."}]},{"target_alias":"SRC_002","attack":true,"case":"The target restricts its claim to tasks where the primary error mode is 'omission rather than misinterpretation,' but the problem asks for an argument about 'tasks with objectively verifiable outcomes' generally—no such restriction. More critically, the problem asks the target to identify what evidence within a single evaluation would distinguish genuine reliability gain from mere checklist compliance. SRC_002's mechanism and claim address why the panel is more reliable (capping variance, preventing omissions) but never identify any evidence within a single evaluation that separates genuine gain from compliance that merely tracks the right answer. The analogy's 'removal of bias spikes' is descriptive, not an evidentiary discriminator. The target is non-responsive to the second half of the problem.","counterexample":[{"scenario":"A panel uses a checklist on an omission-heavy task and gets 95% accuracy. An expert gets 80%. The checklist items are necessary and sufficient to prevent omissions, so the panel's performance is purely mechanical compliance tracking the right answers. Within this single evaluation, the target provides no evidentiary criterion to distinguish this from a case where the panel genuinely improved its underlying judgment."}]}]}